% ==================== ABSTRACT ====================
\begin{abstract}
Automated agricultural report generation presents a fundamental tension between content volume and factual credibility. Standard Large Language Model (LLM) pipelines often prioritize narrative readability, leading to ungrounded speculation, hallucinated market dynamics, and misleading translations. This paper presents the design, evolution, and evaluation of \textbf{MandiBhav by GramIQ}, a highly reliable, data-grounded agricultural intelligence platform. Over five architectural iterations, the platform shifted from an AI-centric content generator into an analytics-driven, trust-calibrated reporting engine. By implementing a strict data-first architecture, local cache fallbacks, dynamic report classification, date-normalization frameworks, and automated claim support validation, the platform eliminates LLM hallucinations and speculation. The final system generates English market reports matching the observed data scope (e.g., \textit{Nagpur APMC} demo mode) and deploys them to GitHub Pages. Factual verification shows zero contradictions or scope violations, ensuring absolute transparency for real-world agricultural stakeholders.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Agricultural intelligence, Large Language Models, data grounding, factual verification, credibility scoring, static site generator, OGD API.
\end{IEEEkeywords}


% ==================== INTRODUCTION ====================
\section{Introduction}

In India, agricultural markets (mandis) operate under high price volatility. Daily price variations directly influence the livelihoods of millions of farmers. Navigating this volatility requires timely and accurate access to market arrivals, minimum support prices (MSPs), and regional modal rates. While the Government of India provides raw market data through the Open Government Data (OGD) API, this raw dataset is difficult for average farmers to interpret.

Large Language Models (LLMs) like Google Gemini offer a promising solution by transforming raw numerical tabular data into intuitive, natural language reports. However, deploying a naive LLM-based agricultural reporting pipeline reveals several challenges in real-world environments:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{API Instability:} Government data endpoints suffer from frequent latency spikes, connection timeouts, and downtime.
    \item \textbf{Factual Hallucinations:} LLMs tend to invent supply/demand dynamics, buyer behaviors, and future price movements that are unsupported by the input price data.
    \item \textbf{Translation Inconsistencies:} Multilingual translation pipelines often alter numeric values, swap units, or introduce translation drift, resulting in conflicting facts.
    \item \textbf{Scope Mismatch:} LLMs frequently generalize localized market observations (e.g., a single market in Nagpur) into national trends.
\end{itemize}

This report documents the architectural journey of \textbf{MandiBhav by GramIQ}. We describe how we refactored a naive content generator into a production-ready, data-grounded agricultural intelligence platform. We outline the design trade-offs, lessons learned, and the implementation of a trust layer that ensures generated reports never exceed the evidence provided by the underlying data.

% ==================== ARCHITECTURE EVOLUTION ====================
\section{Architecture Evolution}

The MandiBhav platform evolved through five distinct architectural phases, transitioning from an AI-centric system to a validation-heavy, data-grounded platform.

\subsection{Version 1: Naive Architecture (AI-Centric)}
The initial prototype relied entirely on the LLM to ingest raw data and generate outputs (Fig.~\ref{fig:arch_v1}). In this model, raw data was pasted directly into the prompt, and the model was tasked with generating English, Hindi, and Marathi articles simultaneously.
