\label{fig:arch_v1}
\end{figure}

This architecture suffered from extreme hallucination rates. Without preprocessing, the LLM invented explanations for price changes, distorted numeric statistics, and suffered from translation drift where Hindi and Marathi articles contradicted the English version. The key lesson was: \textit{AI must not be the source of truth.}

\subsection{Version 2: Overengineered Architecture}
To improve structure, Version 2 introduced a database layer (SQLite) and split the content into multiple report types: National Commodity Reports, State Reports, Market Spotlights, Top Gainers, and Best Markets. 

While structurally richer, a single daily run generated over 20 articles, causing excessive API usage, long execution times ($\ge 5$ minutes), high token costs, and complex debugging. The lesson was: \textit{A reliable, single-report pipeline is worth more than twenty fragile ones.}

\subsection{Version 3: Analytics-Driven Architecture}
Version 3 introduced a dedicated \textbf{Analytics Engine} between the database and the LLM (Fig.~\ref{fig:arch_v3}). Instead of feeding raw records to the LLM, the system first pre-computed averages, Min/Max/Modal spreads, and compared them to historical prices and Minimum Support Prices (MSP). This resulted in a structured ``Fact Package.''
