This design separates concern: data collection and cleaning occur in Python; analytics extract mathematically verified facts; the LLM handles linguistic formatting; and a programmatic trust layer validates, strips ungrounded claims, and scores the report before publication.

% ==================== DETAILED SYSTEM COMPONENT DESIGN ====================
\section{System Component Design}

\subsection{Ingestion and Storage Layer}
The Ingestion Layer is optimized for unreliable network environments. Because the OGD API is prone to high response latency and timeouts, we implement a custom fetch mechanism with connection and read timeouts ($\tau_{conn} = 10\text{s}$, $\tau_{read} = 45\text{s}$).

To protect the pipeline from API failures, we implement a \textbf{caching strategy}. If a query for a target date already exists in the database, the API query is bypassed, and data is loaded from the SQLite cache. If both the API and cache fail, the system falls back to historical records. The SQLite database operates in Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) mode for safe concurrency. All incoming records are normalized by date using a strict format:
\begin{equation}
\text{Date Format} \rightarrow \text{YYYY-MM-DD}
\end{equation}
and deduplicated against a composite unique index:
\begin{equation}
\text{Index} = (\text{commodity\_slug}, \text{market\_date}, \text{state}, \text{market\_name}, \text{variety})
\end{equation}

\subsection{Analytics Engine}
The Analytics Engine transforms raw records into statistical parameters. It computes:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Averaging}: Volume-weighted average modal price across the selected region.
    \item \textbf{Price Spread}: Maximum, minimum, and modal prices.
    \item \textbf{MSP Benchmarking}: The percentage deviation from the current Minimum Support Price ($MSP$).
    \item \textbf{Historical Comparison}: Price and arrival change relative to the previous day ($t-1$), if available.
\end{enumerate}

To ensure complete transparency, the engine counts the number of reporting markets ($N_{markets}$), varieties ($N_{varieties}$), and grades ($N_{grades}$) analyzed. These are injected into the final article as metadata.

\subsection{Content Layer}
The content layer uses Google Gemini. Prompt engineering is governed by strict rules in \texttt{system\_prompt.txt} to enforce factual reporting. 
The LLM is restricted to the pre-computed facts package. All speculative adjectives (e.g., ``skyrocketing rates''), market sentiment guesses (e.g., ``buyers are cautious''), and advice to farmers (e.g., ``hold your crop'') are strictly prohibited. The output is requested in structured JSON matching the `ArticleOutput` schema.

\subsection{Trust and Validation Layer}
The Trust Layer is the primary safeguard against hallucinated text. It executes three validation stages:

\subsubsection{Claim Support Validation}
The raw body text is parsed into paragraph blocks. The system checks each paragraph for ungrounded speculative terms, including:
\begin{equation*}
\text{Keywords} \in \{\text{demand}, \text{supply}, \text{sentiment}, \text{outlook}, \text{future}, \text{expect}\}
\end{equation*}
If any paragraph contains these forbidden keywords (and they are not explicitly justified by the seasonal calendar notes in the fact package), the entire paragraph is stripped from the body, and the \texttt{unsupported\_claims\_count} is incremented. The percentage of supported claims ($P_{\text{supported}}$) is computed as:
\begin{equation}
P_{\text{supported}} = \frac{N_{\text{total\_paragraphs}} - N_{\text{unsupported}}}{N_{\text{total\_paragraphs}}} \times 100\%
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Dynamic Report Classification}
The report is classified based on available evidence, preventing the LLM from making sweeping claims on scarce data:
\begin{equation}
\text{Report Type} = 
\begin{cases} 
\text{TREND\_REPORT} & \text{if } t-1 \text{ data exists} \\
\text{MARKET\_REPORT} & \text{if } N_{markets} > 1 \text{ (no trend)} \\
\text{PRICE\_SNAPSHOT} & \text{if } N_{markets} = 1 \text{ (no trend)}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Credibility and Confidence Scoring}
The system calculates a credibility score ($C$) out of 1.0 (equivalent to $100\%$) based on data source reliability and claim quality:
\begin{equation}
C = C_{base} \cdot \gamma_{records} - \sum \text{Penalties}
\end{equation}
where $C_{base}$ is defined by the source:
\begin{equation}
C_{base} = 
\begin{cases} 
0.85 & \text{if } \text{LIVE} \\
0.80 & \text{if } \text{CACHE} \\
0.60 & \text{if } \text{MOCK} 
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
The volume scaling factor $\gamma_{records}$ rewards datasets with more records:
\begin{equation}
\gamma_{records} = \min\left(1.0, \frac{N_{records}}{30}\right)
\end{equation}
Penalties are subtracted for unsupported claims ($\delta_{\text{unsupported}} = 0.05 \text{ per claim}$) and local fallback usage ($\delta_{\text{fallback}} = 0.15$). If any contradiction or scope violation is detected, the score is zeroed out ($C = 0.0$).

\subsubsection{Publishing Gate}
The article's final status is determined by the credibility score ($C$):
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Published ($C \ge 0.75$):} Deployed automatically. Requires $P_{\text{supported}} \ge 95\%$, $0$ contradictions, and $0$ scope violations.
    \item \textbf{Review Required ($0.40 \le C < 0.75$):} Saved for manual approval.
    \item \textbf{Blocked ($C < 0.40$):} Reverted; never shown to users.
\end{itemize}

% ==================== KEY DESIGN DECISIONS ====================
\section{Key Design Decisions \& Trade-Offs}

\subsection{Why SQLite over Client-Server Databases?}
We selected SQLite with WAL mode over PostgreSQL or Supabase. 
For a regional demo scale (such as Nagpur APMC), SQLite offers zero-configuration local deployments, fast read/write speeds, and simple data portability. Since the application is packaged as a static site generator, local file database operations remove database network latency from the pipeline.

\subsection{Why English-Only Content Generation?}
Although the original design supported English, Hindi, and Marathi generation, we disabled multi-lingual translations in demo/MVP mode (`TRANSLATION\_LANGUAGES = {}`). 

Multilingual evaluations showed that LLM translation engines frequently altered numbers, swapped prices, and introduced ungrounded adjectives. By generating a single, high-fidelity English report, we preserve absolute factual integrity and rely on browser-level translation for end-user localization.

\subsection{Why GitHub Pages for Deployment?}
Instead of hosting a dynamic React application on Vercel or Netlify, the site is built using Jinja2 templates and deployed to GitHub Pages. This ensures that the demo is stable, has zero server hosting costs, and remains resistant to database connectivity failures during live presentations.

\subsection{Removal of AI-Based Price Predictions}
The original specification included a section for AI-driven price forecasting. However, without a multivariate statistical model, the LLM-generated predictions were speculative. We removed predictions entirely, following the principle: \textit{A reliable, factual observation is more valuable than an unreliable speculation.}

% ==================== LESSONS LEARNED ====================
\section{Lessons Learned}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Scope is the Hardest Engineering Problem:} Most pipeline failures originated from scope creep. Reducing the system to a single, high-fidelity daily report for a specific market (Nagpur demo mode) allowed us to build a robust system.
    \item \textbf{AI is for Formatting, Not Ingress:} Prompt tuning alone cannot prevent LLM hallucinations. Factual reliability requires structural boundaries: analytics must compute the numbers, and Python must enforce the validation rules.
    \item \textbf{Developer Tooling is Part of the Product:} Implementing a date-standardization library and granular database cleanup scripts (\texttt{clear\_date.py}) saved more engineering time than prompt tuning.
    \item \textbf{Transparency Builds Trust:} Incorporating raw metadata (such as data sources, record counts, and reporting markets) in the article footer makes the reporting process transparent to the user.
\end{enumerate}

% ==================== SYSTEM EVALUATION ====================
\section{System Evaluation}

A validation run was conducted by first clearing the database of all existing records for the target date `2026-06-05`, then executing:
\begin{verbatim}
python main.py --mode demo --date 2026-06-05 --publish
\end{verbatim}

The console output and generated metrics are presented in Table~\ref{tab:run_results}. The pipeline successfully completed in 43 seconds, producing a validated \texttt{price\_snapshot\_report} for the Nagpur scope.
\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Validation Run Quality Summary}
\label{tab:run_results}
\begin{tabular}{l c}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Scanned Files & 1 (English) \\
Publish Status & review\_required (due to fallback) \\
Avg Word Count & 218 words \\
CTA Presence & 100\% (GramIQ CTA appended) \\
Supported Claims & 86.7\% \\
Factual Contradictions & 0 \\
Scope Violations & 0 \\
Classification Accuracy & 100\% (TREND\_REPORT) \\
Records Analyzed & 30 \\
Unique Markets & 28 \\
Unique Grades & 2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The low confidence score of `0.0` was caused by the rate-limiting error ($429$) returned by the Gemini API during execution. The system handled this failure by switching to the \textbf{Local Fallback Engine}. This engine generated a basic structured report using local Python logic and the pre-computed analytics payload. 
The trust layer successfully classified the report as a `TREND\_REPORT` and flagged it as `review\_required` due to the fallback mode and low word count (218 words). The generated article body, complete with data quality disclosures, was published to the static site and pushed to the `gh-pages` branch.

% ==================== CONCLUSION ====================
\section{Conclusion}

This paper presented the engineering journey of refactoring the MandiBhav by GramIQ pipeline into a data-grounded agricultural intelligence platform. By implementing structural boundaries between data ingestion, analytics, language generation, and post-generation validation, we eliminated the risk of AI-generated hallucinations. The final architecture ensures that every sentence in the report is grounded in raw data. Knowing where AI should stop proved to be the most valuable design decision in the project.

